# Why jQuery Can't Directly Populate `attachments[]` Inputs

You are encountering a fundamental mismatch between how jQuery's `.val()` method works and how HTML forms serialize data for submission. When you execute `$('[name="attachments"]').val(attachArray)`, jQuery attempts to set the value of every element matching that selector to the entire array object. Because an array is an object, it lacks a single string value that can be assigned to the `value` attribute of an `<input>` tag.

In your specific case, the input field is hidden:
```html
<input type="hidden" name="attachments[]">
```

When you pass a JavaScript array directly to `.val()`, jQuery often coerces this into an empty string or fails to set the property correctly depending on the version and browser. Even if you manage to see a value in the DOM inspector, the form serialization logic is the real culprit here. Upon submission, `$_POST['attachments']` expects an array of strings, but the way you are populating the field often results in a single string representation of the array or an empty string, which Laravel interprets as `["attachments"] => [""]`.

The core issue is that you cannot simply dump a JS array into one hidden input and expect the server to reconstruct it. You must construct a valid serialized string that matches the expected format before assigning it to the input's value attribute.

### The Correct Approach: Serialized Arrays

To get an array from JavaScript into a form field named `attachments[]`, you need to serialize the data into a string first. The standard pattern for this is using `JSON.stringify()` combined with a specific delimiter, or utilizing jQuery's built-in serialization utilities if you are dealing with multiple inputs.

However, since you have a single hidden input intended to hold multiple values (a trick often used for file uploads in legacy systems), the most robust method is to create a string that represents the array elements separated by commas or semicolons, depending on how your backend parser handles it.

If your Laravel controller expects a standard PHP array, you usually don't want to send a JSON string at all unless you are explicitly decoding it. Instead, the hidden input should contain the values in a format the server can parse back into an array.

Here is how to correctly set the value:

```javascript
// Assuming attachArray contains file names or IDs as strings
var serializedValue = attachArray.join(','); 
// Or if you need them separated differently:
// var serializedValue = attachArray.map(function(item) { return item; }).join(';');

$('#fileList').html('Selected files: ' + serializedValue);

// Set the value of the hidden input to this string
$('input[name="attachments[]"]').val(serializedValue);
```

### Handling Multiple Inputs Dynamically

If your UI involves dynamically adding new file inputs (e.g., clicking "Browse" adds a new row), you cannot rely on a single static input. In modern implementations, it is better to generate unique names for each file input or use a library that handles dynamic form fields automatically.

If you stick with the single hidden input approach, ensure that the string you assign matches exactly what your server-side code expects when it calls `explode(',', $_POST['attachments[]'])` or similar logic. If your Laravel controller uses `$request->file('attachments[]')`, it expects a specific structure often associated with file uploads where multiple inputs share the same name attribute.

### A Better Pattern for File Attachments

The pattern shown in your HTML snippetâusing a hidden input to aggregate valuesâis common but fragile. It breaks easily when you need to handle multiple files of different types or sizes. A more maintainable approach is to allow multiple distinct `<input type="file">` elements, each with the same name `attachments[]`.

When jQuery detects new inputs being added to the DOM (via AJAX or event listeners), you must update their values individually. You cannot loop over an array and set a single selector once; you must iterate over the inputs themselves.

```javascript
// Example: When adding a new input dynamically
function addAttachmentInput() {
    // Create the new input element
    var newInput = $('<input type="file" name="attachments[]" />');

    // Append to your container
    $('#fileList').append(newInput);
}

// When setting values for existing inputs
attachArray.forEach(function(file, index) {
    $('input[name="attachments[]"]').eq(index).val(file.name);
});
```

### Conclusion on Serialization

The reason you are seeing `["attachments"] => [""]` in your controller is that jQuery's `.val()` with an array object fails to coerce the value into a readable string, resulting in an empty string being assigned. To fix this, serialize the array into a string before assignment, or restructure your form to use multiple inputs if you need to handle dynamic file uploads robustly. Always ensure the format of the data in the `value` attribute aligns with how your backend parses `$_POST`.
